 ## 4. Boundary Discipline
+Be liberal at external boundaries and strict inside Flywheel.
+
+External systems are allowed to be messy: user config, provider streams, LLM tool-call arguments, network responses, SDK callbacks, CLI subprocess output. Boundary adapters may accept recoverable variation, normalize once into Flywheel's canonical typed shape, and surface clear typed errors when semantics are missing or unsafe.
+
+Once data crosses into Flywheel-owned state, Postel's law stops. Current writers, workflow control, and post-recovery execution parse strictly. Historical facts cross one reviewed recovery boundary: parse strictly first, apply only family-owned salvage rules, then emit typed degraded gaps for bytes that remain unreadable. The same canonical recovered history powers transcript display and provider API context, so display and execution never disagree about the readable past. Recorded tool results remain context and are never redispatched.
+
+Use `safeParse` only for external-boundary normalization or the reviewed historical recovery boundary. Use `parse` for all current writes and post-recovery control. Schemas must not carry `.default()` on fields the current producer always sets. Unreadable bytes stay immutable. They never block workspace startup, other sessions, or future appends. A project-wide SQLite or project-directory open failure remains a startup failure.
 
-- Be liberal at external boundaries and strict inside Flywheel. A boundary adapter normalizes external input (user config, provider streams, LLM tool-call arguments, network responses, SDK callbacks, CLI subprocess output) once into Flywheel's canonical typed shape, or surfaces a clear typed error (ARCH-BOUNDARIES-001).
-- Use `safeParse` only for external-boundary normalization or the reviewed historical recovery boundary. Use `parse` for all current writes, workflow control, and post-recovery execution (ARCH-BOUNDARIES-001).
-- Never put `.default()` on a schema field that the current producer always sets (ARCH-BOUNDARIES-004).
-- Read history through the one strict-first recovery boundary, whose recovered history feeds both transcript display and provider API context (ARCH-SUBSTRATE-012). Recorded tool results remain context and are never redispatched (ARCH-SUBSTRATE-038).
-- Keep unreadable bytes immutable. They never block workspace startup, other sessions, or future appends; only a project-wide SQLite or project-directory open failure is a startup failure (ARCH-SUBSTRATE-013).
-- Hold secrets (API keys, OAuth tokens, credentials) as `Redacted.Redacted` everywhere in memory. Unwrap with `Redacted.value` only at the provider-call adapter or one explicit serialization boundary through a plain `to*`/`from*` DTO, and never put a secret in logs, traces, errors, or snapshots (ARCH-BOUNDARIES-009).
-- Keep provider attribution and session identity in fields separate from prompt-cache keys. A cache-disabled request, including compaction, keeps the session headers it needs and emits no prompt-cache key (ARCH-PROVIDERS-026).
-- Prune model config only against the complete authoritative schema or registry, never the slots of a partial read. Correct destructive pruning at its completeness boundary; never disable pruning (ARCH-RUNTIME-069).
+Secrets (API keys, OAuth tokens, credentials) are `Redacted.Redacted` in memory everywhere; unwrap (`Redacted.value`) only at the provider-call adapter or one explicit serialization boundary, which uses a plain `to*`/`from*` DTO so the crossing is intentional. No secret in logs, traces, errors, or snapshots. See ADR-005 "Secret Handling."
+
+Provider attribution and session identity use fields separate from prompt-cache keys. Cache-disabled requests, including compaction, can still require session headers. Preserving identity must not cause prompt-cache keys to be emitted.
+
+Model-config pruning removes deprecated and unknown keys using the complete authoritative schema or registry, never the slots requested by a partial read. Preserve pruning; correct destructive pruning at its completeness boundary rather than disabling it.
 
