+++ b/src/workflows/skills/fly.spec-create/SKILL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+---
+name: Create Spec
+description: Turn a settled design into an implementation-ready spec - exact files, concrete changes, test scenarios, and per-phase verification - grounded in codebase research.
+dependencies:
+  - fly.implementation-spec
+agents:
+  - explorer
+  - advisor
+---
+
+# Implementation Spec Creation
+
+Research the codebase, validate technical claims, and emit a work-ready implementation spec. The method is the `fly.implementation-spec` package composed above; this step adds the role boundary, the grounding sequence, and the artifact contract.
+
+## Role boundary
+
+- Workflow execution keeps target implementation files read-only. `write` and `edit` exist in this step for the scratch spec artifact and for disposable experiments; the spec-create parent owns disposable proof-of-concept tests in workflow scratch and writes them when they produce stronger evidence than static reasoning. A user request to create or edit a target file is downstream implementation work; the read-only target is a role, not a blocker and not an open question.
+- Explorer delegation stays read-only: use explorers for repository research, data analysis, and path probes. Do not dispatch implementer or mutation-capable subagents. Do not ask explorers to create scratch files or proof-of-concept tests.
+
+## Request Triage
+
+The workflow input must be a fully settled design, and it must arrive with no open questions; the chat agent owns every question known before launch. Resolve newly discovered uncertainty by analyzing representative data, running actual code paths, writing disposable proof-of-concept tests in workflow scratch, consulting external documentation, or dispatching explorers before writing the spec. Use `ask_user` only when empirical work cannot answer a narrow user-owned choice, and return a blocked outcome only when repository evidence fundamentally contradicts the approved design and it must be rewritten in chat. If the launch input is incomplete, investigate the missing context here and use `ask_user` only for that narrow user-owned choice: a clarification is not an implementation phase, and `phases: []` is invalid.
+
+Check the `<env>` block's `Repo snapshot` line. A greenfield repository (fewer than 5 tracked files) skips codebase research; go straight to shape synthesis.
+
+## Ground the spec in the codebase
+
+Use the memory the runtime already supplied.
+
+Map each finding to `context`: codebase findings to `key_files[]`, pattern findings to `patterns[]`, doc and external findings to `constraints[]`. Record every project doc consulted (architecture docs, ADRs, guidelines) as repo-relative paths in `context.project_docs[]`, excluding AGENTS.md and source files; downstream review steps forward these paths to reviewers verbatim. Act on the composed research protocol's flags: reuse an `EXISTING_SOLUTION`; treat a `DRY_VIOLATION` as a consolidation hypothesis and add a consolidation task only when the code shares one responsibility and the consolidation has demonstrated benefit while preserving independent boundary obligations; record a rejected consolidation in the existing `context.constraints[]`; record a `PATTERN_CONFLICT` with the reason for keeping or correcting it; cover an `INTEGRATION_RISK` with tests in the affected phase; and resolve an `OPEN_QUESTION`, `CLAIM_INVALID`, or `VERSION_ISSUE` here rather than passing it on.
+
+Before shape synthesis, confirm that the referenced paths exist, that the relevant existing implementations and conventions are known, and that no proposed work duplicates existing code.
+
+Note a confirmed external claim's source inline in the spec text; correct an invalid claim or version conflict by researching a valid implementation shape. Best practices from those sources are candidate constraints for shape synthesis: they enter implementation work only when design-cited or codebase-cited and the selected remedy is the simplest feature-complete shape. An authoritative recommendation alone does not justify new tasks, guards, layers, or scenarios.
+
+## Shape synthesis
+
+Follow the shape-synthesis method in the composed package in your own context; do not dispatch subagents to draft or score alternatives, because the downstream spec-review step is the adversarial check. Record the outcome in one of the package's two required forms as a string appended to `context.constraints[]`, after the user's verbatim description. Do NOT add handoff fields outside the documented schema and do NOT invent ad hoc `context.*` keys for rejected alternatives or scoring metadata.
+
+## Artifact
+
+A completed spec is one JSON document under the workflow scratch `plans/` directory, authored with `write` (and `edit` for bounded extension passes), never through a shell script or heredoc. Its top-level fields are exactly `schema_version: 1`, `summary` (100-5000 chars: the system-level goal and what is being built), `context`, `phases`, `success_criteria`, and `implementation_contract`. Do not emit `open_questions`, `risks`, `notes`, or any other field; risk discussion belongs in `context.constraints[]`. Do not turn discovery, experiments, spikes, or proof-of-concept work into implementation phases. Finish with a `handoff` call carrying only `spec_path`; the host validates the file and reports every problem with its line for you to repair with `edit`. For the rare design-invalidating blocker, emit only `outcome: { state: "blocked", reason, evidence }`.
+
+### `context`
+
+Three arrays plus one optional: `key_files[]` (repo-relative paths with one-line reasons), `patterns[]` (existing patterns to follow), `constraints[]` (pitfalls and design rationale: prerequisites, rejected alternatives, why-this-shape decisions, edge cases), and `project_docs[]` (plain doc paths; omit on greenfield). `context.constraints[0]` is the user's feature description copied literally, prefixed `User feature description (verbatim, authoritative): `; every downstream step treats it as the authoritative constraint. The final `context.constraints[0]` remains the user's verbatim description UNCHANGED, and rationale is appended after it, append-only; never prepend, move, paraphrase, or replace constraints[0]. The `Design intent` section inside it is the fixed tier for every downstream step; every other `constraints[]` entry, every phase, and every directive is implementation design that the implement step may revise with a recorded deviation.
+
+### `phases`
+
+Each phase is `{ id, goal, files, tasks, verification, qualitative_verification }` and each task is `{ id, description, files, test_scenarios }`, with no other fields. `test_scenarios[]` are plain strings that state the expected behavior; `files[]` are plain repo-relative paths.
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "phase-1",
+  "goal": "Land the healthz route and a smoke test.",
+  "files": ["src/server.ts", "tests/server.test.ts"],
+  "tasks": [
+    {
+      "id": "t1",
+      "description": "Add Express GET /healthz returning JSON `{sha: process.env.BUILD_SHA}`.",
+      "files": ["src/server.ts"],
+      "test_scenarios": ["GET /healthz returns 200 with a body containing `sha`"]
+    }
+  ],
+  "verification": "bun run test tests/server.test.ts",
+  "qualitative_verification": null
+}
+```
+
+`verification` is always a runnable command that proves this phase alone: a focused test file, a type or schema smoke check, or a small script, never the project's full-suite commands unless nothing narrower can prove the phase. For a surface one command cannot prove (a server, a rendered view), use a minimal smoke command such as `bun run src/server.ts 2>&1 | grep -q 'listening on'` and put the judgment in `qualitative_verification`. Apply the composed method's phase sizing and never emit a code-review phase: reviewer routing the user requested is a `context.constraints[]` entry for the workflow's code-review step. A UAT phase that drives the running application against acceptance criteria is legitimate implementer-owned verification.
+
+### `success_criteria`
+
+Global acceptance only, including the composed method's elegance criterion anchored to the shape-synthesis rejection. Do not restate phase verification here.
+
+### `implementation_contract` (REQUIRED on every spec handoff)
+
+`implementation_contract` is the implementer's contract: what their diff must commit to. spec-create originates it on every spec handoff and spec-review preserves it; since spec-review is optional, spec-create cannot rely on a later step to add it. Downstream work steps echo every directive in `contract_result`.
+
+Shape: `{ directives: [{ id, text, completion_check }] }`, directives only, 1-4 of them. Each directive is one concrete commitment the diff either satisfies or does not, written so a reader can name what breaks if it were removed, and paired with a `completion_check` that names a file, test, command, or exact symbol the work handoff can cite. Forbidden: exact file allowlists (`phases[].files` already scopes paths), duplicate self-review checks (the implement step already injects plan conformity, diff review, and elegance), generic quality advice such as "follow existing patterns" that is too vague to check, and impossible diff claims such as "only additions" or "no removed lines" when the work needs mechanical imports, exports, wiring, or test setup edits.
+
+```json
+{ "id": "d1", "text": "The /healthz handler reads the build SHA once at startup, not per request.", "completion_check": "tests/server.test.ts asserts two requests return the same SHA after process.env.BUILD_SHA changes mid-run." }
+```
+
+When no commitment beyond the phases is needed, prefer one tight directive naming the highest-risk integration point over a list of weak ones.
+
+## Error handling
+
+Log an explorer failure and continue with the available findings.
